## 2. Subject/oracle separation

The w5 harness (`tools/novacore-evidence.py`, lead-owned, untouched by me)
plants its own token in its own scratch tree under `build/r8-w5/`, and its
scanner is a separate implementation from the test's `_scan_for_token` (it
walks the real system temp and counts unreadable files). Both must hold the
same guarantee, and both passed.

## 3. Break the subject on purpose

Two breaks in `novacore/src/novacore/llm.py` (restoration from the committed
bytes in a finally block; git status after: only the lead's in-flight files,
untouched):

    pristine:
      leak tests exit 0  | OK
      bounded tests exit 0 | OK
    BREAK 1: prompt-file finally-unlink removed
      leak tests exit 1 -> CAUGHT | FAILED (errors=3)
    BREAK 2: incremental 1 KB chunk read replaced by whole-pipe read
      bounded tests exit 1 -> CAUGHT | FAILED (errors=2)

Both headline guarantees are load-bearing: removing the cleanup fails the
timeout/no-leak tests (leftover prompt files), and unbounding the read fails
the memory-budget tests. (For BREAK 2 I first re-derived the author's own
claim independently: `docs/logs/r11-memory-bounding.log` reports peak memory
34 KB on 50 MB of runner output; my re-run of the same -k bounded tests on
the pristine tree confirms the 500 KB budget holds.)

